      "problem": "$\\int (x-{sin}^{2}⁡3x+{cos}^{2}⁡3x)\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x={cos}^{2}⁡3x-{sin}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x-{sin}^{2}⁡3x+{cos}^{2}⁡3x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (x+{cos}^{2}⁡3x-{sin}^{2}⁡3x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
